[{:db/id 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ca37d526-e95e-5c72-ad4b-d05af7166afb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125219 :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n\nII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U,$ we have $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)=p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-42098bc3-a0a9-510c-94ee-f777802bb49a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360475 :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. For any $A\\in R,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in R,$ we have $-1(-1\\cdot A)=-A.$\n\nIII. For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca37d526-e95e-5c72-ad4b-d05af7166afb" :question/id #uuid "ca37d526-e95e-5c72-ad4b-d05af7166afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162394" :question/problem "Let $E$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ that satisfy $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The exponential function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\in E$\n\nII. For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in E.$\n\nIII. The set $E$ is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\nf(0)={e}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\in E.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the function $f(x)=g(x)={e}^{x}\\in E.$ Then, \n\n$$\n(f+g)(0)={e}^{0}+{e}^{0}=1+1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin E.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $E$ is not closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff55dda2-87d0-5c54-864b-9856103ddee8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff55dda2-87d0-5c54-864b-9856103ddee8" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff55dda2-87d0-5c54-864b-9856103ddee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-08624e18-c789-56aa-82f8-d0568d6cacd4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ea867be-0628-5646-98c1-28f732a418d4" "answer-08624e18-c789-56aa-82f8-d0568d6cacd4" "answer-01b5239c-b3c9-5807-b37b-2a5e43bd141d" "answer-d049396a-d59f-556c-a286-aabf743c306b" "answer-96e40a22-66a9-56e3-b03b-cd6284f3fb8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ea867be-0628-5646-98c1-28f732a418d4" :answer/id #uuid "7ea867be-0628-5646-98c1-28f732a418d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08624e18-c789-56aa-82f8-d0568d6cacd4" :answer/id #uuid "08624e18-c789-56aa-82f8-d0568d6cacd4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01b5239c-b3c9-5807-b37b-2a5e43bd141d" :answer/id #uuid "01b5239c-b3c9-5807-b37b-2a5e43bd141d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d049396a-d59f-556c-a286-aabf743c306b" :answer/id #uuid "d049396a-d59f-556c-a286-aabf743c306b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96e40a22-66a9-56e3-b03b-cd6284f3fb8e" :answer/id #uuid "96e40a22-66a9-56e3-b03b-cd6284f3fb8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281525 :question/problem "Let $Q$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is a function $f(x)\\in Q,$ such that $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\nII. For any $f(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n\nIII. For any $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-42098bc3-a0a9-510c-94ee-f777802bb49a" :question/id #uuid "42098bc3-a0a9-510c-94ee-f777802bb49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162666" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in Z.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. For any $A\\in R,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in R,$ we have $2\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\nIII. For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example,the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in R,$ since $4={2}^{2}$ and $2\\in Z.$ However\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\notin R,\n$$\n\nbecause a negative number ($-4$) can't be a square.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $R.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n2\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\notin R\n$$\n\nbecause the $22$-entry of $2\\cdot A$ is not equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd34e2b9-846d-52d4-a7f0-ad33a82569a5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd34e2b9-846d-52d4-a7f0-ad33a82569a5" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd34e2b9-846d-52d4-a7f0-ad33a82569a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-505c9ae0-7f3e-59b3-8123-543ae49a7508" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a3c0dcd-1ed6-5e80-bd76-4898442d0191" "answer-490c90da-97c9-5fee-bb59-bd940c2df35c" "answer-0944afe4-0d0b-565f-af88-3dbcade60f38" "answer-6f7f5023-3192-5bc2-a421-3f0e60a1f07c" "answer-505c9ae0-7f3e-59b3-8123-543ae49a7508"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a3c0dcd-1ed6-5e80-bd76-4898442d0191" :answer/id #uuid "7a3c0dcd-1ed6-5e80-bd76-4898442d0191" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-490c90da-97c9-5fee-bb59-bd940c2df35c" :answer/id #uuid "490c90da-97c9-5fee-bb59-bd940c2df35c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0944afe4-0d0b-565f-af88-3dbcade60f38" :answer/id #uuid "0944afe4-0d0b-565f-af88-3dbcade60f38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f7f5023-3192-5bc2-a421-3f0e60a1f07c" :answer/id #uuid "6f7f5023-3192-5bc2-a421-3f0e60a1f07c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-505c9ae0-7f3e-59b3-8123-543ae49a7508" :answer/id #uuid "505c9ae0-7f3e-59b3-8123-543ae49a7508" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-ad1fad64-3179-5550-8fbd-b2cacb8f6bb0" :activity/id #uuid "ad1fad64-3179-5550-8fbd-b2cacb8f6bb0" :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049737 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-1f027f6f-3170-550b-8b2d-eb37f8a80677" "step-ea8e9bb5-f8d7-5d3d-83dd-b2ae2b6c3647" "step-3b196302-0187-5bfa-8880-a9d96466fbba" "step-a1742ae5-ae23-562a-91d7-bbd1cf427a86" "step-652a43ef-f276-5ff0-8235-02448f8f